 ## Background
-Adaptive step size control originated as a necessity for efficient volume rendering, where fixed step sizes either waste computation in empty space or fail to capture detail in dense regions. Early implementations focused on density-based heuristics for cloud rendering [S1, S2, unofficial]. The variance-based approach was introduced to handle large medical and scientific datasets by precomputing complexity metrics [S1, unofficial]. Interactive adaptive stepping emerged with the need for real-time feedback in visualization tools [S2, unofficial]. Modern implementations often integrate these techniques with temporal accumulation and deep learning-based reconstruction [S2, unofficial].
+The concept of adaptive step sizing in ray marching originated from the need to optimize the computational cost of volume rendering and SDF evaluation. Early implementations focused on safety factors to prevent surface penetration [S2, unofficial]. Modern implementations, such as those found in web-based volume renderers [S1, unofficial] and deep learning-based reconstruction pipelines [S2, unofficial], have expanded these techniques to include variance-based precomputation, dynamic doubling for empty space, and interaction-aware rendering. The method is widely used in real-time volume visualization, medical imaging, and scientific visualization where performance is critical.
 
